<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Sizing: the mark is 54dp wide on the 108dp canvas. Both layers, so the app is the same
  size on the home screen whether themed icons are on or off.

  It used to be 69dp, fitted so the furthest opaque pixel landed on a 72dp circle. That
  was the wrong circle. 72dp is the *square* a mask is guaranteed not to exceed; the shape
  a mask is guaranteed not to cut is the 66dp circle inside it. At 69dp the handshake ran
  tangent to 21% of the launcher's circular mask — measured off a screenshot, not
  inferred — which flattened the light hand's wrist and its fingertips and left the
  near-black background showing only as slivers. It read as a photograph cropped into a
  circle rather than as an icon, and it was visibly bigger than everything beside it.

  54dp is not a guess either. Measured from the app drawer on Manuel's phone, the glyph
  inside each neighbouring icon spans, in these same units: YouTube 62, Authenticator 61,
  Messages 54, BHIM 53, Assistant 50, Camera 49, Calendar 48, Contacts 45, Phone 44.
  A solid diagonal mass reads heavier than an open shape of equal width, so sitting at the
  median of that band rather than its top is what makes this match rather than shout.

  Anything up to about 63dp still clears the 66dp safe circle. The constraint that bit
  here was never clipping — it was looking oversized beside real neighbours, which no
  safe-zone rule detects and only a screenshot does.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
